<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wassily Kandinsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wassily Kandinsky was a Russian-born painter and art theorist, one of the pioneers of abstract art. Settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1896, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-blaue-reiter">Blue Rider</a> group with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-marc">Franz Marc</a> in 1911 and set out his ideas in &quot;Concerning the Spiritual in Art&quot;. He later taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky.jpg" alt="Wassily Kandinsky" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vassily Kandinsky by Adolf Elnain Photo credits : Georges Meguerditchian - Centre Pompidou, MNAM-CCI /Dist. RMN-GP Im... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kandinsky_-_Vassily_Kandinsky_by_Adolf_Elnain_Photo_credits_-_Georges_Meguerditchian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1900 Munich was a leading art city: the Munich Secession was founded in 1892, the magazine Jugend gave its name to German Art Nouveau, and the Blue Rider group of <b>Wassily Kandinsky</b> and Franz Marc formed there in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first teachers were Johannes Itten, Lyonel Feininger and Gerhard Marcks, soon joined by Oskar Schlemmer, Paul Klee and, in 1922, <b>Wassily Kandinsky</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Painters rejected traditional perspective and took colour and shape, not the depiction of nature, as the essence of art; <b>Wassily Kandinsky</b>, Piet Mondrian and Kazimir Malevich sought to redefine art as the arrangement of pure colour.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Neuilly-sur-Seine, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wassily Kandinsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
